 Subgraphs use mermaid's block syntax — `subgraph id [Title]` … `end` — with arbitrary nesting and an optional `direction LR` line per block (inherited from the parent otherwise). A node mentioned inside a block is claimed by it, mermaid-style, even if it first appeared elsewhere. Edges may freely cross cluster borders, and an edge may target a subgraph *itself* (`CF --> VPC`) — it attaches to the cluster box; forward references (edge before the block) work via a pre-scan. See `examples/subgraph.mmd`.
 
-Edges are styled with mermaid's `linkStyle`: `linkStyle 3 stroke:#ff3,stroke-width:4px,color:red` styles the fourth link, `linkStyle 1,2,7 color:blue` several (no spaces in the list), and `linkStyle default …` every link, including ones defined later. Indices count every link in source order, as mermaid does: each pair of a fan-out (`A & B --> C & D` is A-C, A-D, B-C, B-D), invisible `~~~` links, and links to a subgraph; an index past the links defined so far is mermaid's line-numbered "out of bounds" error. Properties: `stroke` (the arrow, cross and circle markers take the same colour), `stroke-width` (0 to 1000px; the arrowhead keeps its normal size, as in mermaid — every end glyph is 11px whatever the stroke, a thick `==>` edge included), `stroke-dasharray` (`5 5`; `0` makes a dotted edge solid), `fill` and `color` (edge label text); unknown properties are ignored, and so is the bare `5` mermaid splits off `stroke-dasharray:5,5`. A later `linkStyle` for the same index replaces the earlier one; the default and an index style merge per property, the index winning. `linkStyle <n> interpolate <curve>` (with or without styles) sets the curve, using the same names as `curve:` above; an `e1@{ curve: … }` on the same edge wins. A `~~~` link stays hidden unless its style sets a positive `stroke-width`, as in mermaid, and never gets markers. Two deliberate differences from mermaid: when both the default and an index style set `stroke`, the markers follow the colour the line is drawn in (mermaid takes the default's), and a circle marker is filled as well as outlined in the edge colour (mermaid colours only its outline).
