 ## Interactivity & desktop apps
 
-Beyond static SVG, the engine exposes an interactive API for GUI apps through the `scene` module: `scene(&graph)` returns a `Scene` — the position, size, and shape of every node plus the bezier curve of every edge in final coordinates — ready to draw with any framework's painter. When a node is dragged, call `route(&graph, &positions)` to re-route edges for custom positions *without* re-running layout — so nodes never jump back. `to_svg(&scene)` exports any state, including after drags. Hit-testing is done by the app from the `Scene` geometry (per-node position + size + shape are all there). An edge ends on its node's border with its end tangent square to the side, so a host draws its own arrowhead there; flowmaid's own SVG stops the stroke half a head short under its 11px head and draws a single curve's last head length straight, painter-only treatments that leave `SceneEdge::bezier` as it is (`Scene::edge_at` follows the painted stroke). Nodes with a mermaid `click` carry it on `SceneNode::link` (url, target, tooltip), so a host can open the link or show the tooltip itself. The same `scene`/`route`/`to_svg` triple exists for ER (`er::`) and class (`class::`) diagrams, and state diagrams ride the flowchart API directly; sequence diagrams and pie charts are static (their `scene()` has no `route()` — nothing sensible to drag).
+Beyond static SVG, the engine exposes an interactive API for GUI apps through the `scene` module: `scene(&graph)` returns a `Scene` — the position, size, and shape of every node plus the bezier curve of every edge in final coordinates — ready to draw with any framework's painter. When a node is dragged, call `route(&graph, &positions)` to re-route edges for custom positions *without* re-running layout — so nodes never jump back. `to_svg(&scene)` exports any state, including after drags. Hit-testing is done by the app from the `Scene` geometry (per-node position + size + shape are all there). An edge ends on its node's border with its end tangent square to the side, so a host draws its own arrowhead there; flowmaid's own SVG stops the stroke half a head short under its 11px head and ends a curve still turning there in a straight run along that tangent, within a pixel of the curve outside the head — painter-only treatments that leave `SceneEdge::bezier` as it is (`Scene::edge_at` follows the painted stroke). Nodes with a mermaid `click` carry it on `SceneNode::link` (url, target, tooltip), so a host can open the link or show the tooltip itself. The same `scene`/`route`/`to_svg` triple exists for ER (`er::`) and class (`class::`) diagrams, and state diagrams ride the flowchart API directly; sequence diagrams and pie charts are static (their `scene()` has no `route()` — nothing sensible to drag).
 
 Consumers built on this API (separate repos; the engine itself stays dependency-free): [flowmaid-desktop](https://github.com/go-routine-id/flowmaid-desktop), an eframe/egui editor with document tabs, a folder explorer, drag & drop canvas with zoom & pan, and SVG export; [flowmaid-web](https://github.com/go-routine-id/flowmaid-web), the wasm playground with the same drag model in the browser; and [**flowcli**](https://github.com/go-routine-id/flowcli), a terminal renderer (ratatui) that draws the `Scene` as Unicode box- and line-drawing — **no SVG at all**, the clearest proof the scene API is genuinely renderer-agnostic. For other frameworks: Tauri/Dioxus can inject the SVG string into a webview; iced has an svg widget; Slint and GTK4 render SVG natively; or draw the `Scene` directly with each framework's painter like the desktop and terminal apps do.
 
